<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="artifacts" title="Artefacts and modules">

    <show-structure for="chapter" depth="2"/>

    <p>
        Nine artefacts are published under <code>%group%</code>: seven jars, the BOM, and
        its parent. Every jar names <code>aether-weaver</code> as its own.
        <code>aether-weaver-bom</code> manages six of the jars' versions; the plugin supplies
        its own. <code>aether-weaver-tests</code> is never installed.
    </p>

    <table style="header-row">
        <tr><td>Artefact</td><td>Holds</td><td>How you get it</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>aether-weaver-api</code></td><td>Annotations, SPI and codes</td>
            <td>With every library below</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>aether-weaver-engine</code></td><td>Weaving pipeline and facade</td>
            <td>With every library below</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>aether-weaver-runtime</code></td><td>Class loader, discovery, config</td>
            <td>Declare it</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>aether-weaver-agent</code></td><td>premain and agentmain</td>
            <td>Declare it; brings runtime</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>aether-weaver-processor</code></td><td>Compile-time checks</td>
            <td>Declare it, provided scope</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>aether-weaver-maven-plugin</code></td><td>The build-time goals</td>
            <td>Declare it as a plugin</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>aether-weaver-testkit</code></td><td>JUnit 5 support, assertions</td>
            <td>Declare it at test scope</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        Runtime, agent, the plugin and the testkit are drivers. The processor is not: it only
        reads a class file, and never writes one. A driver decides when a class is woven and
        where its bytes come from. <a href="choose-a-driver.topic">Choose a driver</a>
        compares the four, and <a href="determinism.topic">Determinism</a> is why the choice
        does not change the output.
    </p>

    <chapter title="What depends on what" id="graph">
        <p>
            Each arrow is a <code>dependency</code> element in that module's pom.
        </p>

        <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    plugin["Maven plugin"] --> engine["Engine"]
    processor["Processor"] --> engine
    testkit["Testkit"] --> engine
    agent["Agent"] --> runtime["Runtime"]
    runtime --> engine
    processor --> api["API"]
    engine --> api
        </code-block>

        <p>
            Adding a driver brings the engine and the api with it. The agent also brings the
            runtime. <code>org.jetbrains:annotations</code> is managed at
            <code>provided</code>, so it reaches no consumer classpath. The testkit's JUnit is
            <code>provided</code> too, and you supply your own; <a href="testing-woven-code.topic">Testing
            woven code</a> covers that.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="The bill of materials" id="bom">
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="aether-weaver-bom">
                <p>
                    Import it with <code>&lt;type&gt;pom&lt;/type&gt;</code> and
                    <code>&lt;scope&gt;import&lt;/scope&gt;</code>. The six library artefacts
                    then need no version of their own.
                    <a href="install.topic">Install</a> has the stanza inside a pom that builds.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="aether-weaver-maven-plugin">
                <p>
                    <code>dependencyManagement</code> never versions a
                    <code>&lt;plugin&gt;</code> element, the BOM's included; only
                    <code>pluginManagement</code> does. Give it its own
                    <code>&lt;version&gt;</code>.
                    <a href="maven-goals.topic">Maven goals</a> has the goals it binds and the
                    parameters they take.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Rules the build enforces" id="rules">
        <p>
            Each of these fails <code>mvn verify</code> rather than a review.
            <a href="building-and-testing.topic">Building and testing</a> is how you run them.
        </p>

        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="The dependency arrow points one way">
                <p>
                    <code>ProjectStructureTest</code> reads the <code>import</code> lines of
                    every published module. The api may name no other module. The engine may
                    name no driver. No driver may name another, and the agent importing the
                    runtime is the one exception the test allows.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Engine internals stay in the engine">
                <p>
                    A package path holding <code>.internal.</code> carries no compatibility
                    promise. <code>ProjectStructureTest</code> scans imports for it: no
                    published module but the engine may import one, and no <code>public</code>
                    or <code>protected</code> line in the engine may name one by its simple
                    name. A fully qualified reference, or a driver's own <code>internal</code>
                    package, escapes that scan.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="One bytecode library">
                <p>
                    Rewriting goes through <code>java.lang.classfile</code>. The enforcer bans
                    ASM, Javassist, Byte Buddy and cglib in every module, tests included.
                    AssertJ's Byte Buddy is excluded from the dependency rather than exempted
                    from the rule.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="No module descriptor">
                <p>
                    Checkstyle rejects a file named <code>module-info.java</code>, and the test
                    walks the tree for one. Every published jar carries
                    <code>Automatic-Module-Name</code> instead: <code>%group%</code> plus the
                    artefact's last segment, except the Maven plugin, which ends
                    <code>.maven</code>.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The IntelliJ plugin is outside the reactor">
                <p>
                    <code>aether-weaver-ide</code> holds a Gradle build and no
                    <code>pom.xml</code> at its root, and the reactor never lists it. Building
                    it downloads an IntelliJ Platform distribution, which
                    <code>mvn install</code> must not need.
                    <a href="intellij-plugin.topic">The IntelliJ IDEA plugin</a> is what it produces, and
                    <a href="repository-layout.topic">Repository layout</a> is where the rest of the tree sits.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

